-- title:
--   Mod m functionals on the Hecke algebra realised by cusp forms
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $m$ a nonzero natural number. Write $\mathbb{T} =$ [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra p 2 ∅`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) for the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb{C}}\bigl(S_2(\Gamma_0(p))\bigr)$ generated by the operators $T_\ell$ for all primes $\ell \nmid p$ together with the operators $U_q$ for all primes $q \mid p$ (the excluded set of primes being empty), and write $a_n(g) =$ [`ModularFormClass.qCoeff g n`](def/FLTPrelim_Modularity.html#L19), the $n$-th coefficient of the $q$-expansion of $g$ at the cusp $\infty$. Call a cusp form $g \in S_2(\Gamma_0(p))$ $q$-integral when $a_n(g)$ lies in the smallest subring of $\mathbb{C}$ for every $n$, i.e. all its $q$-expansion coefficients are rational integers; this is the set [`CuspForm.qIntegralSet p`](def/CuspForm_IntegralLattice.html#L11). Assume [`CuspForm.HasIntegralBasis p`](def/CuspForm_IntegralLattice.html#L17), that is, the $\mathbb{C}$-span of the $q$-integral cusp forms is all of $S_2(\Gamma_0(p))$. Let $\varphi : \mathbb{T} \to \mathbb{Z}/m$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-linear map. Then there is a $q$-integral cusp form $f$ such that for every $t \in \mathbb{T}$ and every integer $c$ with $(c : \mathbb{C}) = a_1(tf)$, the residue of $c$ in $\mathbb{Z}/m$ equals $\varphi(t)$. (The conclusion is phrased as an implication about integers $c$ representing $a_1(tf)$; such a $c$ exists because the Hecke algebra preserves integrality of $q$-expansions.)
--
--   This is the duality between the Hecke algebra of weight $2$ level $p$ and the $\mathbb{Z}$-lattice of cusp forms with integral $q$-expansion, in the form used in Mazur's computation with the Eisenstein ideal: the pairing $(t, g) \mapsto a_1(tg)$ is perfect over $\mathbb{Z}$, so every $\mathbb{Z}/m$-valued functional on $\mathbb{T}$ is induced by a $q$-integral cusp form. It is used by [`CuspForm.exists_qIntegral_eisenstein_eigen_mod_of_injective`](thm.html#CuspForm.exists_qIntegral_eisenstein_eigen_mod_of_injective) to produce cusp forms congruent mod $m$ to a prescribed Hecke eigensystem.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_CuspForm_exists_qIntegral_qCoeff_apply_one_eq_of_hasIntegralBasis.lean

import Definitions.Def_CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra
